=== E4.1: The 'Novel -> Bad -> Win' Game-Playing Example (Section 3.1, Figure 1, pp. 17-18) ===
  After 9 [Bad->0] and 1 [Novel->Bad->1] episodes (true Markov E[z|Novel]=0.10, E[z|Bad]=0.10):
    Widrow-Hoff TD(1): P(Novel) = 1.0000, P(Bad) = 0.1000  (|P(Novel) - 0.10| = 0.9000)
    Linear TD(0):      P(Novel) = 0.1000, P(Bad) = 0.1000  (|P(Novel) - 0.10| = 0.0000)

=== E4.2: Non-Markovian Counterexample Where TD(0) Fails and TD(1) Succeeds (p. 18) ===
  Under non-Markovian history dependence (true E[z | Novel] = 1.00):
    Linear TD(0) prediction for Novel:      0.1000 (error = 0.9000 — misled by Markov state aliasing!)
    Widrow-Hoff TD(1) prediction for Novel: 1.0000 (error = 0.0000 — unbiased Monte Carlo return!)

